The == operator in Java is used to compare the values of two objects, not the references to the objects themselves. It is a reference-based comparison, meaning that it checks if the values of the objects are equal.

On the other hand, the .equals() method is used to compare the contents of two objects, not their references. It checks if the contents of the objects are equal, regardless of their references.

The use of == is generally discouraged in Java programming because it is not a reliable way to compare objects. It can lead to unexpected behavior and errors in your code.

In your case, using == instead of .equals() is a good practice because it is a more reliable way to compare strings in Java. It ensures that you are comparing the values of the strings, not their references.

