String str2 = "Hello";

if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal.");
}
```

In this example, the == operator is used to compare the two strings, and the result is printed to the console.

On the other hand, here's an example of how to compare two strings using .equals():

```java
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "Hello";

if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("The strings are equal.");
} else {
    System.out.println("The strings are not equal.");
}
```

In this example, the .equals() method is used to compare the two strings, and the result is printed to the console.

In summary, == is generally discouraged in Java programming because it is not a reliable way to compare objects. It is recommended to use .equals() instead to compare the contents of two objects, regardless of their references.

